{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed168776016/1/codegen_prompts/6.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed168776016/1`\n- 题号：`6`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed168776016/1/6.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"6\">\n- `description`：将网格中的多个同色对象视为\"拼图碎片\"，按照隐藏在背景噪声中的位置标记将它们拼合成一个完整图形，再用碎片重叠次数决定输出颜色深浅。\n- `Input structure`：背景色为黑色。网格中有若干用蓝色绘制的 4-连通对象（形状各异，不重叠），以及散布在背景中的若干红色单像素标记点。红色标记点的数量与蓝色对象的数量相同。\n- `Rule`：\n  1. 将每个蓝色对象与一个红色标记点配对：每个蓝色对象的边界框中心（取整到最近像素）与最近的尚未配对的红色标记点配对。\n  2. 将每个蓝色对象平移，使其边界框中心移动到其配对的红色标记点位置。\n  3. 平移后，多个蓝色对象可能发生重叠。输出网格中：没有覆盖的位置保持黑色；被恰好一个对象覆盖的位置着蓝色；被两个对象重叠覆盖的位置着绿色；被三个或更多对象重叠覆盖的位置着黄色。\n  4. 红色标记点在输出中被擦除。\n- `Key insight`：红色标记点不是装饰或边界，而是每个碎片的目标锚点——通过\"中心-最近点\"的配对逻辑将碎片聚合。第二层规则是重叠计数到颜色的映射：1→蓝、2→绿、3+→黄，这需要从多个样例中归纳出来。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/247ef758, eval/a395ee82, eval/271d71e2, eval/dfadab01, train/e39e9282, train/57edb29d, train/ba1aa698, eval/581f7754\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"247ef758\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.4151\nmechanism=0.5473\nlexical=0.0149\ncode_feature=0.3333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:frame, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:translate\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/247ef758.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/247ef758.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nMove colored sprites from the left strip into a framed area at marker-defined targets.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background; rectangular frame on right with colored markers on its edges; sprites in a narrow left strip separated by a constant-color divider column.\n\nRule:\n- For each color, target rows/cols are where both opposite frame edges share that color. Targets are the Cartesian product.\n- Erase each sprite from the left and stamp it centered at every target. Higher sprites cover lower ones.\n\nKey insight: One sprite replicates to a grid of row × column marker intersections.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"sprite s in the left area whose color appears on the right frame\",\n  \"dst\": \"center at positions d in the right frame\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.color == d.same_row_frame_edges.color and s.color == d.same_col_frame_edges.color\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"move sprites from bottom to top, with new covering old\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a395ee82\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3998\nmechanism=0.4777\nlexical=0.0150\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:counter, object:marker, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:translate, relation:overlap\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/a395ee82.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/a395ee82.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nStamp translated copies of a monochrome template guided by a singleton pixel layout.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. A large monochrome shape (template, color T).\n- Singleton pixels elsewhere: exactly one is color T (layout origin); the rest share color O (overlap color).\n- Singletons sit on a uniform-spacing lattice.\n\nRule:\n- Convert each singleton's offset from the origin into coarse lattice units.\n- Place a template copy at each offset, scaled by template height/width.\n- The origin's copy is painted O instead of T.\n- Any pixel covered by two or more copies is also recolored to O.\n\nKey insight: The single T-colored singleton identifies both the origin position and the \"self\" copy, which is uniquely recolored.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"marker pixel set s with different colored center pixel and peripheral pixels\",\n  \"dst\": \"monochrome sprite d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.center.color == d.color\",\n  \"transform\": \"s.peripheral_pixels -> d. d.color -> s.peripheral_pixels.color\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"271d71e2\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3343\nmechanism=0.3815\nlexical=0.0145\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:counter, object:frame, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:translate\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/271d71e2.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/271d71e2.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nMove bordered containers toward same-colored destination markers, charging gray cells to orange.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = 6 (magenta). Black-bordered (0) rectangles with gray (5) / orange (7) interiors.\n- A head sprite (9) sits adjacent to one container edge; a matching destination sprite lies along the movement axis.\n\nRule:\n- Each container translates toward its destination along the head-to-dest axis.\n- Steps = min(head-to-dest distance, number of gray interior cells).\n- Gray cells become orange from the head-facing side inward, one per step.\n\nKey insight: Gray cell count caps movement — a container stops short if it runs out of gray cells to charge.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"rectangular container with head that has same color as destination line\",\n  \"path\": \"head forward\",\n  \"dst\": \"reach the destination line or move_distance == s.inner_gey_pixels.num\",\n  \"type2\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src2\": \"inner grey pixels whose num == move_distance\",\n  \"transform\": \"change color to orange\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"grey pixels change color in order\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dfadab01\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3387\nmechanism=0.4496\nlexical=0.0150\ncode_feature=0.2500\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, object:frame, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:sprite, operation:translate\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/dfadab01.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/dfadab01.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nExpand each colored control pixel into its corresponding fixed 4x4 glyph.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with single-pixel markers in colors 2, 3, 5, and/or 8.\n- Some inputs also show a sample 4x4 glyph in the output color, with a same-rule marker just below-right of the sample.\n\nRule:\n- Replace each marker color by its learned 4x4 glyph: 2->yellow frame, 3->blue ring, 5->pink diagonal blocks, 8->orange X.\n- Paint each glyph with its top-left corner at the marker pixel, clipping at the grid boundary.\n- Remove the sample glyphs and skip their below-right label markers.\n\nKey insight:\n- The colored pixels are control pixels for a fixed glyph alphabet; non-control sample glyphs are only legend entries, not preserved scene objects.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"single-pixel markers in the glyph alphabet colors\",\n  \"transform\": \"replace each marker by its fixed 4x4 glyph, clipped at grid edge; sample glyphs and their below-right labels disappear\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e39e9282\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3230\nmechanism=0.4000\nlexical=0.0152\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:sprite, operation:translate, relation:nearest, relation:overlap\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/e39e9282.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/e39e9282.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nProject maroon markers into adjacent grey or pink 3x3 squares, erasing grey squares afterward.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background, solid 3x3 grey and/or pink squares, and maroon marker pixels or segments directly touching one side of exactly one square.\nRule\nGrey squares are erased; their adjacent maroon markers stay in place and stamp the nearest edge cell inside the grey square. Pink squares stay pink; their adjacent maroon markers disappear and stamp the corresponding cell on the square's middle row or middle column.\nKey insight\nThe side touched by a marker determines the projection: grey uses the near edge, while pink uses the centerline, preserving the marker's coordinate along that side.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"maroon marker pixels or segments adjacent to solid grey or pink 3x3 square objects\",\n  \"transform\": \"erase grey squares after stamping maroon into each touched near-edge cell; keep grey-adjacent markers in place; keep pink squares, remove pink-adjacent markers, and stamp their maroon cells onto the matching middle row or column of the pink square\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"57edb29d\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3240\nmechanism=0.3662\nlexical=0.0146\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:counter, object:frame, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:sprite, operation:translate, relation:nearest, relation:overlap\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/57edb29d.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/57edb29d.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nExtract the single plain rectangle and redraw it with the aligned rectangles' border-relative marker pattern.\n\nInput structure:\n- The boundary color is the background/separator.\n- Foreground objects are dense rectangular regions; exactly one rectangle has only its base color.\n- Other rectangles contain their base color plus marker pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Crop the only plain rectangle; this crop is the output canvas.\n- Use marked rectangles whose row span overlaps the plain rectangle's row span.\n- Transfer their marker pixels by preserving each marker run's distance from the nearest rectangle edges.\n\nKey insight:\n- The marked rectangles are examples of an edge-relative pattern, so the missing plain rectangle receives the same pattern resized to its own dimensions.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"edge-relative marker run s in a marked rectangle aligned with the plain rectangle row band\",\n  \"dst\": \"run d in the plain rectangle crop\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.nearest_edge_offsets == d.nearest_edge_offsets\",\n  \"transform\": \"paint each matched d with the shared marker color; keep base rectangle fill elsewhere\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"same shape as the plain rectangle\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ba1aa698\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3288\nmechanism=0.4087\nlexical=0.0146\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:counter, object:frame, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:translate, relation:nearest\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/ba1aa698.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/ba1aa698.json\n</paths>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
